#
# Caddy reverse proxy for agrent.
#
# ── THIS FILE IS NOT DEPLOYED, AND IT IS NOT DRIFT-CHECKED ────────────────
#
# `deploy/apply.sh` copies ONLY docker-compose.vm.yml to the VM, and
# `deploy/check-drift.sh` hashes only that file. The Caddy container mounts
# `./Caddyfile` from /opt/agrent, and that copy is edited ON THE VM by hand.
# Editing this file changes nothing in production. It is the repo's record of
# what the live file should contain, and it is reconciled by hand in both
# directions — with nothing checking that the reconciliation happened.
#
# ── WHAT IS AND IS NOT VERIFIED (#842) ────────────────────────────────────
#
# VERIFIED, in this repo, 2026-09-10: the `caddy` service in
# deploy/docker-compose.vm.yml declares NEITHER `env_file` NOR `environment`
# — unlike `app` and `worker`, which both carry `env_file`. So nothing in
# this repo can put ACME_EMAIL into the Caddy container, and the
# `{$ACME_EMAIL:admin@agrent.bg}` placeholder below resolves to its default
# whenever this file is what runs. An earlier revision of this header claimed
# "it also sets ACME_EMAIL in the environment, overriding the default below";
# that claim was not true of anything in the repo and has been withdrawn.
#
# NOT VERIFIED: what the live /opt/agrent/Caddyfile actually contains, and
# what ACME account email the running Caddy is using. Neither was reachable
# from where this was written, so no value is asserted here and none was
# guessed. `email` only controls Let's Encrypt expiry notices; certificate
# issuance does not depend on it.
#
# TO CLOSE THIS: diff the live file against this one, and then either extend
# `deploy/apply.sh` + `deploy/check-drift.sh` to cover the Caddyfile (so the
# divergence becomes detectable) or delete this copy and stop implying the
# repo is its source of truth. Until one of those happens, treat every line
# below as UNCONFIRMED against production.
#
# `tests/guardrails/caddyfile-divergence.test.ts` holds the parts of the
# above that a machine can check.
#
# The site block serves the custom domain `app.agrent.bg` alongside the
# sslip.io host. Let's Encrypt issues a cert automatically on first request
# for each name in the site block.
#

{
    # Use the production LE endpoint by default (default behaviour).
    # ACME_EMAIL is NOT supplied by anything in this repo — the `caddy`
    # service has no `env_file` and no `environment` — so this resolves to
    # the default below unless the VM's own copy of this file differs. See
    # the header.
    email {$ACME_EMAIL:admin@agrent.bg}
}

35-187-80-26.sslip.io, app.agrent.bg {
    encode gzip zstd

    # Client-body limit roughly matches FILE_MAX_SIZE_BYTES above.
    request_body {
        max_size 110MB
    }

    # Security headers.
    header {
        Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains"
        X-Content-Type-Options    "nosniff"
        X-Frame-Options           "DENY"
        Referrer-Policy           "strict-origin-when-cross-origin"
        -Server
    }

    reverse_proxy app:3000 {
        # Next.js sits behind the proxy — forward the original host so
        # NextAuth + server-side redirects resolve to the public URL.
        header_up Host {host}
        header_up X-Real-IP {remote_host}
        header_up X-Forwarded-For {remote_host}
        header_up X-Forwarded-Proto {scheme}
    }
}
